| §D.D11 — Shadow-tomography sample / query complexity for π(x) extraction (Aaronson 2018; Huang-Kueng-Preskill 2020 classical shadows): under the random-Rademacher (= random-Pauli-Z) classical-shadow query model `y_j := ⟨σ_j, χ_P⟩` with σ_j ~ Unif({±1}^N), how many queries K suffice to estimate π(M) for ALL M ∈ [1, N] simultaneously to ε accuracy? Cumulative-window indicator `1_{[1, M]}` is a **global observable** in the random-Rademacher ensemble; HKP shadow norm `‖O‖²_shadow ≤ 4^k · ‖O‖∞²` for k-body local observables makes cumulative windows have shadow norm `Θ(M)`. **Theorem 1 (closed form)**: linear unbiased estimator `π̂(M; K) = (1/K) Σ_j y_j · ⟨σ_j, 1_{[1, M]}⟩` is unbiased with `Var[π̂(M; K)] = (M·π(N) − π(M) + π(M)² − π(M)) / K ~ M·π(N)/K` (leading order). Proof: expand four-σ moment; under n ≠ m, n' ≠ m' constraints only pairings (B) n=n', m=m' and (C) n=m', m=n' contribute; B sum = M·π(N) − π(M), C sum = π(M)² − π(M). **Corollary (query lower bound)**: for ε-accuracy `max_{M ≤ N dyadic} | π̂(M) − π(M) | ≤ ε` w.p. ≥ 1−δ across all log₂N dyadic windows (Chebyshev + union), `K ≥ Ω(N · π(N) · log(N/δ) / ε²) = Ω̃(N²/log²N · ε^{-2})`. For ε=1, `K = Θ̃(N²)` — strictly polynomial in N, NOT polylog. Direct sieving solves the same task in O(N log log N) ops; shadow oracle is asymptotically WORSE than direct sieving by factor `π(N) / (log²N · log log N)`. **A-grade target (polylog QUERY complexity for π) RULED OUT** within classical-shadow framework. **Empirical verification** at N = 2¹⁵ = 32 768, π(N) = 3 512, M ∈ {32, 64, ..., 32768}, K ∈ {10², 3·10², 10³, 3·10³, 10⁴, 3·10⁴, 10⁵} with n_trials ∈ {5, ..., 20}: empirical std at every (K, M) within ±30% of theoretical (within finite-trial Monte Carlo noise); L∞ error scales as `K^{−0.50}` matching CLT prediction; extrapolated K_* for L∞ ≤ 1 satisfies `K_*/(N·π(N)) ≈ 1`. **Adds EDGE E1.11** — random-Rademacher shadow query complexity of π(M) is `Θ̃(N · π(M))` per simultaneous-M target. **Third computational axis** (random-mask oracle / sample complexity) the project has explored, orthogonal to E1.5 (info rate), E5.x (circuit size), E6.x (time / space). **Distinct from**: E1.5 (incremental per-step entropy of `π mod m` — adaptive single-modulus chain; this bounds non-adaptive batch query across all M); E5.6 (deterministic non-uniform circuit size; this is randomised query); E6.6 (Aggarwal adaptive binary-search with EXACT π queries; this is non-adaptive random-mask only); E6.7 (Meissel-Lehmer time-space deterministic). **No reshuffling of orthogonal random ensembles** (Rademacher → Gaussian → Walsh-Hadamard) changes the Θ(M) shadow norm of cumulative-window indicators. **Successor entries** (added inline as §D.D11.a/b/c): D11.a Walsh-Hadamard structured shadow (predicted closure, M²/K residual scaling forces K_WH = Ω(N)); D11.b Möbius / Liouville shadow (potentially conditional A-grade under RH via L∞ residual `O(M^{1/2 + ε})` of `Σ λ`); D11.c Bernoulli-iid density-matched control (B-grade duplicate of theorem). **Cross-domain ingredient**: HKP 2020 *Nature Physics* 16, 1050 = arXiv:2002.08953; Aaronson 2018 STOC = arXiv:1711.01053. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §8 row "Holographic / shadow tomography" promoted PROPOSED → USED-E. Cites E1.5 (info-content axis), E2.21 (parity major-arc Walsh-Hadamard L∞ — D11.a successor), E5.6 (circuit size), E6.6 (Aggarwal adaptive). **Channelled mathematician**: Aaronson — sample / query complexity in randomised computational models; framing in his style; answer (Ω̃(N²) — strictly polynomial) shows HKP shadow-tomography "few-measurements" speedup does NOT extend to classical global observables. See `experiments/information_theory/{shadow_tomography_chi_p.py, shadow_tomography_chi_p_results.md, shadow_tomography_chi_p_data.json}`, `archive/sessions/session226_d11_shadow_tomography_chi_p.md`. | E (cross-domain HKP technique survives import; cumulative-window observable is structurally global so polylog speedup is absent) | E | 226 |
