## Summary

**Session 226 — D11 Shadow tomography of χ_P (cross-domain attack mode), B-grade closure.**

**Cross-domain import (USED-E):** Huang-Kueng-Preskill 2020 classical-shadow protocol with random-Pauli-Z (= random-Rademacher) ensemble. The HKP shadow-norm bound `‖O‖²_shadow ≤ 4^k · ‖O‖∞²` for k-body local observables — vs `Θ(4^n)` for global observables — was the structural axis I exploited.

**Theorem 1 (closed form).** For the linear unbiased estimator `π̂(M; K) := (1/K) Σ_j ⟨σ_j, χ_P⟩ · ⟨σ_j, 1_{[1,M]}⟩`:
```
Var[π̂(M; K)] = (M·π(N) − π(M) + π(M)² − π(M)) / K  ~  M · π(N) / K.
```
Proved by direct expansion of the four-σ moment.

**Corollary.** `K ≥ Ω(N · π(N) · log(N/δ) / ε²) = Θ̃(N²)` for ε = 1 simultaneous accuracy across all dyadic M ≤ N. Direct sieving achieves the same in `O(N log log N)` — shadow oracle is asymptotically WORSE.

**Empirical verification at N=2¹⁵, K∈{10²,...,10⁵}:** L∞ scaling fits `K^{−0.483}` (theoretical −0.5); extrapolated K_*/(N·π(N)) = 1.74 — within constant factor of theoretical Θ(N·π(N)).

**Adds EDGE E1.11** — third computational axis (random-mask oracle / sample complexity) orthogonal to E1.5 (information rate), E5.x (circuit size), E6.x (time / space).

